    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 1 (Questions 1&ndash;6)</div>\n<p>Quantitative reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-qPCR) is the primary method for analyzing gene expression in molecular oncology. Total cellular RNA is extracted and reverse-transcribed into complementary DNA (cDNA) using an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase. The resulting cDNA is amplified across thermal cycles comprising denaturation, primer annealing, and extension. Target abundance is monitored cycle-by-cycle via fluorescence detection chemistries.</p>\n<p>Two main fluorescence systems are employed: non-specific intercalating dyes and sequence-specific fluorogenic hydrolysis probes. SYBR Green I binds to the minor groove of all double-stranded DNA (dsDNA), emitting strong fluorescence upon intercalation. However, because SYBR Green detects any dsDNA, post-amplification dissociation (melting curve) analysis is mandatory to evaluate amplicon purity. As temperature steadily increases from 65&deg;C to 95&deg;C, dsDNA denatures into single strands, causing an abrupt drop in fluorescence at the melting temperature ($T_m$). Specific amplicons yield a sharp, single melting peak, whereas low-$T_m$ peaks indicate non-specific artifacts such as primer-dimers.</p>\n<p>In contrast, TaqMan probes are sequence-specific dual-labeled oligonucleotides containing a 5' reporter fluorophore (such as 6-FAM) and a 3' quencher dye (such as TAMRA). In an intact probe, F&ouml;rster resonance energy transfer (FRET) suppresses reporter emission. During primer extension, the 5'-to-3' exonuclease activity of <em>Taq</em> DNA polymerase cleaves the annealed probe, physically releasing the reporter from the quencher to generate signal proportional to amplicon accumulation.</p>\n<p>Researchers investigated the oncogenic drivers <em>MYC</em> and <em>VEGFA</em> in glioblastoma cell lines treated with vehicle or the pan-histone deacetylase inhibitor panobinostat (2 &mu;M), with or without <em>EZH2</em> small interfering RNA (siRNA). Target transcript levels were normalized against the endogenous reference gene glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (<em>GAPDH</em>) using the Livak $2^{-\\Delta\\Delta C_t}$ method, where $\\Delta C_t = C_{t,\text{target}} - C_{t,\text{GAPDH}}$, and $\\Delta\\Delta C_t = \\Delta C_{t,\text{treated}} - \\Delta C_{t,\text{control}}$ (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 RT-qPCR threshold cycles, fold expression changes, and amplicon melting temperatures across glioblastoma treatments</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Treatment Condition</th><th>Target Gene</th><th><em>GAPDH</em> Mean <em>C_t</em></th><th>Target Mean <em>C_t</em></th><th>&Delta;<em>C_t</em></th><th>&Delta;&Delta;<em>C_t</em></th><th>Fold Expression (2<sup>&minus;&Delta;&Delta;<em>C_t</em></sup>)</th><th>Amplicon <em>T_m</em> (&deg;C)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Vehicle Control</td><td><em>MYC</em></td><td>18.2 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>22.2 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>+4.0</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.00 (Reference)</td><td>85.5 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Panobinostat (2 &mu;M)</td><td><em>MYC</em></td><td>18.1 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>25.1 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>+7.0</td><td>+3.0</td><td>0.125</td><td>85.4 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Panobinostat + <em>EZH2</em> siRNA</td><td><em>MYC</em></td><td>18.3 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>28.3 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>+10.0</td><td>+6.0</td><td>0.0156</td><td>85.5 &plusmn; 0.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Vehicle Control</td><td><em>VEGFA</em></td><td>18.2 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>21.2 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>+3.0</td><td>0.0</td><td>1.00 (Reference)</td><td>83.2 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Panobinostat (2 &mu;M)</td><td><em>VEGFA</em></td><td>18.2 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>23.2 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>+5.0</td><td>+2.0</td><td>0.250</td><td>83.1 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Non-Template Control (NTC, SYBR)</td><td><em>MYC</em> Primers</td><td>&gt; 40.0</td><td>36.8 &plusmn; 0.6</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td><td>Undetectable</td><td>71.8 &plusmn; 0.4</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
