        "e": "<strong>Selectable antibiotic resistance markers in plasmid vectors (Topic 639):</strong> Plasmid cloning vectors must contain three essential structural features: (1) an origin of replication (*ori*) ensuring autonomous replication inside bacterial hosts; (2) a multiple cloning site (MCS) containing unique restriction enzyme recognition sites for insert integration; and (3) a **selectable marker**, typically an antibiotic resistance gene such as the kanamycin resistance gene ($Kan^R$, encoding aminoglycoside 3'-phosphotransferase). Transformation is an inherently inefficient process, with only a small fraction of competent *E. coli* cells successfully taking up a plasmid molecule. When the transformation mixture is plated on growth medium containing kanamycin, the antibiotic binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit of untransformed bacteria, causing translational misreading and cell death. Only bacterial cells that successfully took up the pET-28a plasmid express aminoglycoside 3'-phosphotransferase, which phosphorylates and inactivates kanamycin, allowing selective colony formation.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes housekeeping or metabolic genes; untransformed wild-type *E. coli* divide normally on standard non-selective media.<br>&bull; C describes promoter elements; the antibiotic resistance gene is a selectable marker, not the promoter for the cloned gene (which is the T7 promoter).<br>&bull; D describes molecular chaperones; antibiotic resistance genes confer resistance to antibiotics, not prevention of protein aggregation."
