    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>The clinical identification of somatic and germline mutations in oncology guides targeted therapeutic selection. In non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), activating mutations within the epidermal growth factor receptor (<em>EGFR</em>) tyrosine kinase domain\u2014such as the L858R point mutation in exon 21\u2014confer sensitivity to first-line tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs, e.g., erlotinib). However, secondary acquired gatekeeper mutations, notably T790M in exon 20, sterically hinder inhibitor binding and drive drug resistance.</p>\n<p>Clinical laboratories evaluated two distinct diagnostic modalities for detecting <em>EGFR</em> mutations: automated Sanger dideoxy chain-termination sequencing and high-throughput Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS, Illumina sequencing-by-synthesis). Sanger sequencing utilizes four dideoxynucleotide triphosphates (ddATP, ddCTP, ddGTP, ddTTP), each labeled with a distinct fluorophore. Chain termination occurs when a ddNTP lacking both 2' and 3' hydroxyl groups is incorporated by DNA polymerase into a growing complementary strand, halting extension. The resulting terminated fragments are separated by high-resolution capillary gel electrophoresis, and fluorescence emission is detected to construct an electropherogram.</p>\n<p>In contrast, Illumina NGS executes massively parallel solid-phase sequencing on a glass flow cell. Genomic DNA is fragmented, ligated to synthetic adapters, and immobilized on a lawn of oligonucleotides. Isothermal bridge amplification generates dense clonal clusters of identical DNA molecules. During each sequencing cycle, DNA polymerase incorporates reversible dye-terminators containing a fluorophore and a chemically cleavable 3'-O-azidomethyl blocking group. Following laser imaging of all clusters simultaneously, the fluorescent tag and 3' block are enzymatically cleaved, exposing a free 3'-hydroxyl group for the subsequent nucleotide addition cycle.</p>\n<p>Tumor re-biopsies from three NSCLC patients progressing on erlotinib were analyzed by both technologies (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Diagnostic EGFR mutation profiling, analytical sensitivity, and variant allele frequencies (VAF) across NSCLC biopsies</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Patient Sample</th><th>Tumor Cell Purity (%)</th><th>Sanger Electropherogram Findings</th><th>NGS Read Depth</th><th>NGS Variant Allele Frequency (%)</th><th>Diagnostic Mutation Call</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Patient 1</td><td>65%</td><td>Dual T/G peak at codon 858; single C peak at 790</td><td>1,250&times;</td><td>L858R: 42.4%; T790M: 0.0%</td><td>*EGFR* L858R Sensitizing Only</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Patient 2</td><td>45%</td><td>Dual T/G peak at codon 858; dual C/T peak at 790</td><td>1,800&times;</td><td>L858R: 31.8%; T790M: 28.5%</td><td>*EGFR* L858R + T790M Resistant</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Patient 3</td><td>15%</td><td>Single T peak at codon 858; single C peak at 790</td><td>2,400&times;</td><td>L858R: 8.6%; T790M: 4.1%</td><td>*EGFR* L858R + T790M Resistant</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Limit of Detection</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>~15&ndash;20% Mutant Allele Frequency</td><td>&ge; 500&times;</td><td>~1.0% Mutant Allele Frequency</td><td>NGS &gt; 15&times; Higher Sensitivity</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
