        "e": "<strong>Biochemical mechanism of dideoxynucleotide (ddNTP) chain termination (Topic 640):</strong> Normal canonical deoxynucleotide triphosphates (dNTPs) lack a hydroxyl group at the 2' carbon of the ribose ring (possessing hydrogen) but maintain a reactive **3'-hydroxyl (-OH) group**. During continuous DNA polymerization, the 3'-OH group of the terminal nucleotide executes a nucleophilic attack on the $\u0007lpha$-phosphate of the incoming dNTP, releasing inorganic pyrophosphate ($PP_i$) and forming a 3'-to-5' phosphodiester bond. Dideoxynucleotide triphosphates (ddNTPs) are synthetic nucleotide analogs that lack hydroxyl groups at **both the 2' and 3' carbons** of the pentose sugar. When DNA polymerase incorporates a ddNTP into the nascent strand, the absence of a 3'-OH group eliminates the nucleophile required to attack the next incoming dNTP. Chain elongation is thus irreversibly halted at that specific base position.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes ribonucleotide chemistry (RNA has 2'-OH, DNA lacks 2'-OH; ddNTP lacks both 2'-OH and 3'-OH).<br>&bull; B is a Chemical Fabrication trap; magnesium acts as a catalytic divalent cofactor for polymerase coordination, not as a covalent terminator.<br>&bull; D is a Mechanism Misattribution trap; while fluorophores are attached to the base, termination is caused chemically by the absence of the 3'-OH group, not steric hindrance by the fluorophore."
