        "e": "<strong>Reading automated Sanger sequencing electropherograms (Topic 640):</strong> In automated dye-terminator Sanger sequencing, capillary gel electrophoresis separates fluorescently labeled, single-stranded DNA fragments based on molecular mass and length. Because DNA molecules possess a uniform negative charge-to-mass ratio along their phosphate backbone, smaller fragments navigate through the polymer sieving matrix faster than larger fragments. The smallest resolved fragment represents the primer plus a single incorporated fluorescent ddNTP at the 5' end of the newly synthesized complementary strand. As fragments elute past the laser detector, a spectrophotometer records the fluorescence emission wavelength over time, generating a four-color chromatogram. Therefore, reading peaks in order of capillary elution\u2014from **smallest to largest molecular fragment**\u2014yields the exact nucleotide sequence of the nascent complementary DNA strand in the **5'-to-3' direction**.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B is a Directional Inversion trap; capillary electrophoresis elutes the smallest fragments first (nearest the 5' primer), so reading from largest to smallest reads 3' to 5'.<br>&bull; C is a Misattribution trap; peak height reflects incorporation efficiency and dye quantum yield, not the number of hydrogen bonds (A-T has 2, G-C has 3).<br>&bull; D is a Physical Separation Misconception; DNA molecules possess a constant charge-to-mass ratio; capillary sieving separates fragments strictly by length/molecular sieving."
