        "e": "<strong>Genomic DNA sequencing vs cDNA (RNA-seq) transcriptome profiling (Topic 640):</strong> Eukaryotic structural genes contain alternating coding exons and non-coding introns, surrounded by upstream transcriptional promoters and enhancers. During eukaryotic gene expression, pre-mRNA is transcribed from genomic DNA and undergoes post-transcriptional processing in the nucleus: 5' 7-methylguanosine capping, 3' polyadenylation, and **pre-mRNA splicing** by the spliceosome. Splicing removes all **intronic sequences**, ligating the mature exons together. When complementary DNA (cDNA) is synthesized from mature cytoplasmic mRNA using reverse transcriptase, the cDNA reflects only the processed mRNA sequence. Therefore, sequencing cDNA omits non-coding introns, promoter regions, enhancer elements, and intergenic chromosomal DNA. In contrast, genomic DNA sequencing captures all exon-intron junctions, deep intronic variants, promoters, and structural chromosomal boundaries.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes exonic coding sequences, which are preserved in mature spliced mRNA and cDNA.<br>&bull; B describes 5' UTR sequences, which are retained in mature mRNA transcripts.<br>&bull; D describes polyadenylation motifs located in the 3' UTR of mature mRNA, which are retained in cDNA."
