    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>The Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2 (<em>HER2</em> / <em>ERBB2</em>) gene encodes a 185 kDa transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinase. Overexpression of HER2 occurs in approximately 20% of invasive breast carcinomas, promoting aggressive tumor proliferation and metastatic progression. To investigate whether elevated HER2 expression stems from chromosomal gene amplification, enhanced transcription, or post-translational stabilization, researchers employed three classical macromolecular blotting methodologies.</p>\n<p>Southern blotting was performed to assess genomic gene copy number. Genomic DNA was isolated from breast cell lines, digested with the restriction endonuclease <em>HindIII</em>, resolved by agarose gel electrophoresis, depurinated with dilute acid, and denatured with alkaline 0.5 M NaOH solution. The resulting single-stranded DNA fragments were transferred by capillary action onto a positively charged nylon membrane, cross-linked with ultraviolet radiation, and hybridized with a $^{32}\text{P}$-radiolabeled cDNA probe complementary to human <em>HER2</em> exon 4. The single-copy housekeeping gene <em>GAPDH</em> served as an internal diploid reference.</p>\n<p>Northern blotting evaluated mRNA transcript length and abundance. Total cellular RNA was resolved on a formaldehyde-denaturing agarose gel to disrupt secondary RNA secondary structures, transferred to a nylon membrane, and probed with the radiolabeled <em>HER2</em> probe.</p>\n<p>Western blotting quantified protein expression and receptor autophosphorylation. Whole-cell protein lysates were prepared in sample buffer containing sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) and &beta;-mercaptoethanol, resolved via SDS-PAGE, and transferred onto polyvinylidene difluoride (PVDF) membranes. Blots were probed with primary antibodies against total HER2 and phosphorylated HER2 (p-Tyr1248), followed by horseradish peroxidase (HRP)-conjugated secondary antibodies and chemiluminescent detection.</p>\n<p>Four breast epithelial cell models were benchmarked: non-malignant MCF-10A, luminal MCF-7, HER2-amplified SK-BR-3, and SK-BR-3 cells treated with the humanized anti-HER2 monoclonal antibody trastuzumab (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Southern, Northern, and Western blotting metrics across breast epithelial cell models</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cell Model &amp; Treatment</th><th>Southern: *HER2* / *GAPDH* DNA Ratio</th><th>Northern: Transcript Size &amp; Relative mRNA</th><th>Western: Total HER2 Protein (185 kDa, Rel. OD)</th><th>Western: Phospho-HER2 (p-Tyr1248, Rel. OD)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>MCF-10A (Non-malignant)</td><td>1.0 &plusmn; 0.1 (Diploid)</td><td>4.8 kb (1.0 &plusmn; 0.1)</td><td>1.0 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>0.8 &plusmn; 0.1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>MCF-7 (Luminal Breast Cancer)</td><td>1.1 &plusmn; 0.1 (Diploid)</td><td>4.8 kb (1.4 &plusmn; 0.2)</td><td>1.5 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>1.2 &plusmn; 0.1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>SK-BR-3 (Vehicle Control)</td><td>8.4 &plusmn; 0.6 (Amplified)</td><td>4.8 kb (12.2 &plusmn; 0.9)</td><td>15.8 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>18.4 &plusmn; 1.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>SK-BR-3 + Trastuzumab (24 h)</td><td>8.3 &plusmn; 0.5 (Amplified)</td><td>4.8 kb (11.9 &plusmn; 0.8)</td><td>6.2 &plusmn; 0.5</td><td>2.1 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
