        "e": "<strong>Comparative taxonomy of blotting methodologies (Topic 641):</strong> Macromolecular blotting techniques are distinguished by target macromolecule, gel matrix, and detection probe (classic mnemonic: **SNOW DROP** = Southern/DNA, Northern/RNA, Western/Protein):<br>&bull; **Southern blotting:** Separates restriction-digested **DNA** fragments on agarose gels and detects target sequences via hybridization with complementary labeled nucleic acid probes (cDNA or oligonucleotides).<br>&bull; **Northern blotting:** Separates cellular **RNA** on denaturing (formaldehyde) agarose gels to prevent secondary structure folding and detects specific transcripts and transcript sizes via hybridization with complementary nucleic acid probes.<br>&bull; **Western blotting:** Separates denatured **proteins** on polyacrylamide gels (SDS-PAGE) based on molecular weight and detects specific target antigens using primary antibodies and enzyme-conjugated secondary antibodies.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes Western blotting (proteins probed with antibodies), not Southern blotting.<br>&bull; C describes Southern blotting (genomic DNA probed with complementary nucleic acids), not Western blotting.<br>&bull; D describes post-translational modification analysis or non-specific protein staining, not standard macromolecular blotting."
