        "e": "<strong>Signal amplification via enzyme-conjugated secondary antibodies in Western blots (Topic 641):</strong> In Western blotting, detection can be direct (enzyme conjugated directly to primary antibody) or indirect (unlabeled primary antibody recognized by an enzyme-conjugated secondary antibody). The primary technical advantage of the two-step indirect method is **signal amplification**. A primary antibody (e.g., rabbit IgG) binds with high affinity to a specific epitope on the target protein via its variable Fab regions. When a secondary antibody (e.g., goat anti-rabbit IgG-HRP) is introduced, it recognizes conserved antigenic epitopes on the constant fragment (**Fc domain**) of the primary antibody. Because each primary IgG molecule contains multiple antigenic sites in its Fc region, several secondary antibody molecules can bind to a single primary antibody molecule. Each secondary antibody carries horseradish peroxidase (HRP), catalyzing multiple rounds of luminol oxidation, thereby exponentially increasing chemiluminescent light output.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Physical Impossibility trap; proteins are immobilized on the membrane surface, and antibodies do not permeate through solid plastic.<br>&bull; B is a Chemical Misconception trap; antibody-antigen interactions rely on non-covalent forces (hydrogen bonds, ionic, hydrophobic), not covalent bonds.<br>&bull; C is a Protocol Inversion trap; blocking non-specific sites with BSA or milk is equally mandatory when using secondary antibodies to prevent high background."
