        "a": 2,
        "e": "<strong>Structural requirements for Sandwich ELISA: non-overlapping epitopes (Topic 642):</strong> A double-antibody Sandwich ELISA functions by forming a molecular 'sandwich' around the target antigen: solid-phase capture antibody &bull; antigen &bull; detection antibody. For this configuration to form successfully, the target antigen must possess **at least two distinct, non-overlapping epitopes** (antigenic determinants) that are sterically accessible simultaneously. The immobilized capture antibody binds to Epitope 1 (e.g., amino acids 27\u201340 of cTnI), anchoring the antigen to the microtiter well. The detection antibody must bind to a completely separate, non-overlapping Epitope 2 (e.g., amino acids 87\u201399 of cTnI). If an antigen is a small hapten or possesses only a single epitope, binding of the capture antibody sterically blocks the detection antibody from accessing the antigen, making Sandwich ELISA impossible and requiring competitive ELISA instead.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Biochemical Fabrication trap; antigens do not need catalytic prosthetic groups; peroxidase is conjugated to the detection antibody/streptavidin.<br>&bull; B is an Overconstraint trap; monomeric proteins (like cTnI, 24 kDa) are easily measured by sandwich ELISA as long as they have two distinct epitopes.<br>&bull; D describes substrate-converting enzymes; most ELISA targets (like troponin, hormones, cytokines) are structural or signaling proteins without peroxidase activity."
