        "e": "<strong>Immuno-affinity enrichment and sensitivity of Sandwich ELISA (Topic 642):</strong> In human plasma or serum, high-abundance proteins (such as serum albumin, ~40 mg/mL, and immunoglobulins, ~10 mg/mL) exceed the concentration of cardiac troponin I (< 10 pg/mL) by a factor of over $10^9$. In Direct ELISA, unpurified patient serum is applied directly to the microtiter well. Because plastic adsorption is non-specific and driven by general hydrophobic interactions, high-abundance serum proteins coat virtually the entire plastic surface, sterically crowding out the trace cTnI molecules (antigen crowding effect). Consequently, very few cTnI molecules adhere, resulting in poor sensitivity (LLOD = 450 pg/mL). In contrast, Sandwich ELISA pre-coats the wells with high-affinity monoclonal capture antibodies. When plasma is added, the capture antibodies specifically bind and **affinity-purify** the low-abundance cTnI molecules directly from the complex serum matrix, while non-specific competing proteins are washed away, lowering the detection limit to 4.2 pg/mL.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B is an Action Misattribution trap; direct coating on polystyrene does not destroy primary amino acid epitopes, but highly abundant serum proteins (albumin, globulins) crowd out rare antigens.<br>&bull; C is a Biochemical Fabrication trap; HRP is not allosterically inhibited by serum albumin.<br>&bull; D describes radioimmunoassays (RIA), whereas ELISA relies on enzyme-linked chromogenic turnover (HRP converting TMB)."
