        "e": "<strong>IPTG induction and allosteric derepression in the bacterial T7 expression system (Topic 645):</strong> In recombinant protein expression strains such as *E. coli* BL21(DE3), the gene encoding bacteriophage T7 RNA polymerase is integrated into the bacterial chromosome under the control of the *lacUV5* promoter, while the target gene of interest is cloned on an expression plasmid (such as pET) under the control of a T7 promoter containing an adjacent *lac* operator. In the absence of an inducer, the endogenous **LacI repressor protein** binds tightly to the *lac* operator sequences, physically blocking host RNA polymerase and suppressing basal transcription of T7 RNA polymerase. **IPTG (isopropyl &beta;-D-1-thiogalactopyranoside)** is a synthetic, non-hydrolyzable allosteric analog of allolactose. When added to the bacterial culture, IPTG diffuses into the cell and binds with high affinity to the LacI repressor. This binding induces an allosteric conformational change in LacI that causes it to dissociate from the operator DNA. Freed from repression, host RNA polymerase transcribes T7 RNA polymerase, which then transcribes enormous quantities of target recombinant mRNA. Because *E. coli* &beta;-galactosidase cannot metabolize or cleave IPTG, the inducer concentration remains constant throughout growth.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes a protein kinase substrate; IPTG is an isopropyl thiogalactoside sugar analog, not a phosphate donor.<br>&bull; C describes ribosomal initiation factor interactions; IPTG regulates transcription initiation at the promoter, not translation initiation at the Shine-Dalgarno sequence.<br>&bull; D describes protease inhibitors (e.g. PMSF, EDTA); IPTG has no intrinsic antiprotease activity."
